{:now [{:claim "Constructible polygons are a statement about field degrees"
        :modern "The regular n-gon is constructible with ruler and compass exactly when Euler's phi(n) is a power of 2, i.e. n = 2^a times distinct Fermat primes; Gauss' periods are the fixed fields of the subgroups of (Z/nZ)*, the first worked example of Galois theory."
        :tex "[\\mathbb{Q}(\\cos\\tfrac{2\\pi}{n}):\\mathbb{Q}] = \\tfrac{\\varphi(n)}{2} = 2^k"}
       {:claim "Curvature is intrinsic"
        :modern "Gaussian curvature depends only on the metric (the first fundamental form) and is invariant under local isometries; Riemann extended it to the curvature tensor of any dimension."
        :tex "K = \\frac{R_{1212}}{EG - F^2}"}]
 :fields [{:field :abstract-algebra :how "The 17-gon's tower of quadratics is the subgroup chain of a cyclic group of order 16, the template of Galois correspondence." :example "Only five Fermat primes are known: 3, 5, 17, 257, 65537."}
          {:field :differential-geometry :how "Intrinsic curvature founds differential geometry: geodesics, parallel transport and the Gauss-Bonnet theorem." :example "The angle sum of a geodesic triangle exceeds pi by the curvature integrated over it."}
          {:field :mechanics-physics :label "Physics" :how "General relativity measures gravity as the curvature of a metric, the Theorema egregium in four dimensions." :example "Light bending near the Sun, 1919."}
          {:field :mechanics-physics :label "Engineering" :how "No map of the sphere keeps all distances, since K = 1/R^2 cannot be bent to 0; every projection trades one distortion for another." :example "Mercator keeps angles and inflates Greenland."}
          {:field :logic-computing :label "Computing" :how "Discrete Gaussian curvature (angle defect at mesh vertices) drives mesh processing, parametrisation and surface flattening." :example "Flattening a 3D garment pattern into cloth panels."}]
 :tangents [{:series :group-theory :hook "Gauss' periods are the subgroups of (Z/17Z)*; the same idea decides the solvability of the quintic."}
            {:series :physics :hook "From the first fundamental form to Riemann's metric and Einstein's field equations."}
            {:series :erlangen :hook "Isometries keep K: curvature as an invariant of a transformation group, Klein's programme in one example."}]
